const ACRONYMS_HTML=`<h3 id="lab-glossary">Terms and abbreviations</h3><dl>
<dt>2D / 3D</dt><dd>Two-dimensional / three-dimensional geometry: a planar domain / geometry with elevation as well as horizontal position.</dd>
<dt>DDA</dt><dd>Digital differential analyzer: a traversal that advances between the next crossed grid boundaries. Here it visits every height-grid cell crossed by a line of sight.</dd>
<dt>IQR / CI</dt><dd>Interquartile range / confidence interval. The interquartile range describes variation among observer cases; the bootstrap confidence interval estimates uncertainty in the suite median under its stated sampling design.</dd>
<dt>pp</dt><dd>Percentage points: the arithmetic difference between two percentages, rather than a percentage change relative to one of them.</dd>
<dt>CPU / GPU</dt><dd>Central processing unit / graphics processing unit. The visibility solvers and benchmark here run as JavaScript on the central processor; browser display is a separate operation.</dd>
<dt>CGAL</dt><dd>Computational Geometry Algorithms Library, a separate library cited for published algorithms and complexity bounds. This page does not run that library.</dd>
<dt>BVH</dt><dd>Bounding volume hierarchy: a tree of enclosing boxes used to reject groups of triangles or edges before testing them individually.</dd>
<dt>LOS</dt><dd>Line of sight: the straight segment from the observer to a target. Visibility requires no intervening opaque geometry.</dd>
<dt>TIN / DEM</dt><dd>Triangulated irregular network / digital elevation model. Here terrain is a network of planar triangles. A raster elevation model samples a grid and needs an interpolation rule between samples.</dd>
<dt>GRASS</dt><dd>Geographic Resources Analysis Support System, the geographic software whose terrain-grid viewshed documentation is cited below.</dd>
<dt>JIT</dt><dd>Just-in-time compilation: a browser can compile frequently executed JavaScript while the page runs, changing timing after warmup.</dd>
<dt>PNG / JSON</dt><dd>Portable Network Graphics / JavaScript Object Notation: the raster image export format / the text format for saved settings and benchmark results.</dd>
<dt>SVG / HTML</dt><dd>Scalable Vector Graphics / HyperText Markup Language: editable vector diagram elements / the page's controls and selectable explanations.</dd>
<dt>IEEE 754</dt><dd>The floating-point standard of the Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers. <a href="https://tc39.es/ecma262/multipage/ecmascript-data-types-and-values.html#sec-ecmascript-language-types-number-type" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">JavaScript numbers use its binary64 format</a>; rounding errors remain, and tolerances are not a proof of geometric robustness.</dd>
<dt>SIGGRAPH / JASA / SDH</dt><dd>Special Interest Group on Computer Graphics and Interactive Techniques / Journal of the Acoustical Society of America / Spatial Data Handling. These abbreviations identify conferences or publications in the sources.</dd>
<dt>ID</dt><dd>Identifier: an integer linking a result fragment or camera cell to its original receiver triangle. It records ownership rather than a geometric quantity.</dd>
</dl>`;
const SOURCES_HTML=`<p><b>Continuous 2D visibility:</b> Bungiu, Hemmer, Hershberger, Huang &amp; Kröller (2014), <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1403.3905" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Efficient Computation of Visibility Polygons</a>; the <a href="https://doc.cgal.org/latest/Visibility_2/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Computational Geometry Algorithms Library (CGAL) visibility manual</a>. The lab implements its own opening-based propagation, not a binding to that library. The <a href="https://doc.cgal.org/latest/Triangulation_2/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">triangulation manual</a> describes other point-location strategies, including walks and triangulation hierarchies; the lab uses a bounding-box tree.</p>
<p><b>Shadow boundaries:</b> McGuire, <a href="https://developer.nvidia.com/gpugems/gpugems/part-ii-lighting-and-shadows/chapter-9-efficient-shadow-volume-rendering" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Efficient Shadow Volume Rendering</a>; Stich, Wächter &amp; Keller, <a href="https://developer.nvidia.com/gpugems/gpugems3/part-ii-light-and-shadows/chapter-11-efficient-and-robust-shadow-volumes-using" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Efficient and Robust Shadow Volumes</a>. These discuss silhouette extrusion and stencil-based rendering. Here the central processing unit (CPU) clips receiver polygons directly; no stencil counts or graphics processing unit (GPU) timing supplies the answer.</p>
<p><b>Convex-cell beams:</b> Funkhouser et al., <a href="https://www.cs.princeton.edu/~funk/sig98.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">A Beam Tracing Approach to Acoustic Modeling for Interactive Virtual Environments</a>, 1998, and <a href="https://gfx.cs.princeton.edu/gfx/pubs/Funkhouser_2004_ABT/index.php" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">A Beam Tracing Method for Interactive Architectural Acoustics</a>, 2004. The first is a computer-graphics conference paper; the second appears in the Journal of the Acoustical Society of America. They provide the general cell/beam principle. This lab independently adapts it to direct visibility above a terrain mesh, without acoustic reflections or diffraction.</p>
<p><b>Terrain and projected surface context:</b> De Floriani &amp; Magillo, <a href="https://www.geos.ed.ac.uk/~gisteac/gis_book_abridged/files/ch38.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Intervisibility on terrains</a>; Suleiman, Joliveau &amp; Favier, <i>A New Algorithm for 3D Isovist</i> (2012), supplied Spatial Data Handling conference manuscript, <i>SDH2012.pdf</i>, pages 9–12; Chang &amp; Park (2011), <a href="https://doi.org/10.5659/AIKAR.2011.13.3.19" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Exploration of Isovist Fields to Model 3D Visibility With Building Facade</a>. These are conceptual context; the lab's clipped terrain fragments and camera transformation are its own implementation.</p>
<p><b>Ray acceleration and raster terrain:</b> <a href="https://pbr-book.org/4ed/Primitives_and_Intersection_Acceleration/Bounding_Volume_Hierarchies" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Physically Based Rendering: Bounding Volume Hierarchies</a> explains group rejection during ray traversal. <a href="https://grass.osgeo.org/grass-stable/manuals/r.viewshed.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Geographic Resources Analysis Support System (GRASS) r.viewshed</a> documents a terrain-grid sweep with bilinear elevation interpolation. Its sweep differs from this lab's planar occupancy-grid traversal and individual line-of-sight queries through the bilinear terrain grid.</p>
<p><b>Isovist measures:</b> Hosseini Alamdari et al. (2022), <a href="https://doi.org/10.1177/23998083221083680" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">New algorithms for generating isovist field and isovist measurements</a>; Pech, Lam &amp; Masek (2020), <a href="https://ro.ecu.edu.au/ecuworkspost2013/9356/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Quantifiable Isovist and Graph-Based Measures for Automatic Evaluation of Different Area Types in Virtual Terrain Generation</a>. Visible volume, projected occupation and physical visible surface area are distinct measures. The patch and camera-cell surface estimators illustrated here are lab implementations.</p>`;
const COST_HTML=`<p>Choose an output first: a continuous plan polygon, visible physical terrain fragments, a viewport image, or a grid mask. These outputs have different preparation and reconstruction costs. A precise first-hit intersection does not make the region between sampled directions precise. <a href="#cost" data-scene="cost" data-variant="bench">Benchmarks</a> compares matched queries across balanced sampled suites, while the linked examples below explain individual calculations.</p>
<p>Here n denotes boundary segments, t terrain triangles, R sample directions, P raster cells, F surviving fragments and K processed beam entries. These are workload parameters, not interchangeable operations. Published complexity bounds apply to their specified algorithms; this JavaScript implementation uses floating-point clipping and is not certified exact arithmetic.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Method and example</th><th>What drives work</th><th>When to use it</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><a href="#subtraction" data-scene="subtraction" data-variant="subtract">Subtract wall shadows · Subtract shadows, wall by wall</a></td><td>Every wall considers the surviving convex pieces. A coarse model is O(nFv), where v is vertices per piece; F and v grow with splitting. Footprint removal is separate preparation, repeated by fresh calls here.</td><td>Small polygonal scenes, interpretable continuous cuts, or inputs without a reusable triangulation. Long walls can remove broad regions; many obstacles can create many fragments.</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="#expansion" data-scene="expansion" data-variant="expand">Expanding triangles · Locate → expand the complete isovist</a></td><td>Locate q, then propagate surviving openings through mesh adjacency. Work follows reached entries and clipping complexity; holes can create repeated entries. Embedded triangulation and point-location indexing are reusable preparation.</td><td>Repeated observers in static floor plans and footprints, especially where walls terminate most branches. A modified map requires updating its mesh and adjacency.</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="#air" data-scene="air" data-variant="expand">Terrain: beams · Accumulate all terrain beams</a></td><td>Build a conforming air-cell mesh, then process K entries with polygonal apertures and side planes. A cell can be reached through several disjoint openings. The implementation guards excessive entry growth.</td><td>Continuous terrain fragments and compartmentalized space. Open landscapes or fine decompositions can keep many beams alive; mesh preparation and memory matter.</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="#terrain" data-scene="terrain" data-variant="all">Terrain: shadow cuts · Subtract shadows, face by face</a>; <a href="#shadows" data-scene="shadows" data-variant="subtract">Shadow construction · Subtract shadows, face by face</a></td><td>Occluder-first convex differences. All-pairs starting work can be O(t²); subsequent cost depends on surviving receiver fragments and vertices. Half-space rejection saves full clips, but the teaching implementation has limited spatial/angular culling.</td><td>Continuous triangle-interior boundaries and physical surface measures on manageable meshes. Building receivers include walls and roofs; terrain is a height field. Their different domains should not share a speed ranking.</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="#projection" data-scene="projection" data-variant="accumulate">Terrain in the view · Build the observer view, triangle by triangle</a></td><td>Clip every candidate receiver against a physical viewport frustum, then apply the same depth-aware shadow kernel receiver first. Fully rejected blockers still require tests; a smaller viewport reduces output and some work.</td><td>A specified observer-facing viewport and clipped physical surfaces within it. Its counters describe a subset of the scene, not a cheaper full viewshed.</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="#metrics" data-scene="metrics" data-variant="raycast" data-dimension="2d">Geometry vs Raster · Raycast (2D)</a>; <a href="#metrics" data-scene="metrics" data-variant="raycast" data-dimension="3d">Raycast (3D)</a></td><td>Brute force costs O(Rn) or O(Rt). A bounding volume hierarchy rejects groups, with worst cases still testing all primitives. Reuse the index across observers. Physical 3D area also needs connected terrain-sheet recovery inside the angular cells, including shared-edge traversal and clipping.</td><td>Individual line-of-sight queries and sampled surfaces. A hierarchy speeds intersections; connected terrain recovery fills source-triangle ownership gaps, while finite angular boundary error remains.</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="#metrics" data-scene="metrics" data-variant="raster" data-dimension="2d">Geometry vs Raster · Raster (2D)</a></td><td>Classify P occupancy cells, then walk crossed grid cells for each free query cell. Naive full-grid querying on a roughly square grid can require O(P√P) visits. Obstacles and output are quantized.</td><td>A planar occupancy mask with explicit resolution. Thin walls and narrow gaps can disappear or thicken; center classification is not conservative geometry.</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="#metrics" data-scene="metrics" data-variant="raster" data-dimension="3d">Geometry vs Raster · Raster (3D)</a></td><td>Prepare raster elevation nodes and their original-mesh texture pieces. Query each texel center by traversing crossed cells and testing the bilinear surface against the sightline. Classify its full texture footprint. Work grows with query texels and crossed cells; preparation is reusable for one geometry/resolution.</td><td>A terrain visibility texture with complete visible/hidden coverage. Relief and classification are approximated by the grid; physical area is measured on the original triangles. This is central-processor terrain-grid code, not graphics-hardware rasterization.</td></tr>

</tbody></table>
<h3>Related methods, not demonstrated here</h3><p>The Computational Geometry Algorithms Library describes a linear query for a simple polygon without holes, a rotational sweep with O(n log n) work for polygons with holes, and triangular expansion after reusable triangulation. The lab's independent corner-event reference scans walls at each event interval and has O(n²) intersection work; it remains a numerical cross-check, with no dedicated tab. These published bounds do not describe every step of this implementation.</p><p>Geographic Resources Analysis Support System (GRASS) <code>r.viewshed</code> instead computes a terrain-grid viewshed using a sweep that reuses neighboring queries and reports O(P log P) work. Its bilinearly interpolated elevation-grid sweep differs from the individual digital differential analyzer queries used by Raster here, and from planar occupancy and angular Raycast. Graphics processing unit (GPU) depth/stencil methods, hierarchical scene culling, robust exact predicates and parallel implementations can change practical rankings; none is timed by this lab.</p>
<h3>Geometry, resolution and amortization</h3><p>Broad walls and ridges can block whole geometric regions at once. Narrow gaps, many holes and frequent depth changes demand either many samples or many fragments. A feature of width w at distance d subtends roughly w/d radians; resolving two angular dimensions can require sample counts proportional to (d/w)². This is intuition, not a guaranteed error bound. Halving raster cell width quadruples cell count for a fixed planar domain. Cube cells are uniform in camera coordinates, not in solid angle.</p><p>For Q observers on one fixed model, compare preparation + Q × query + requested reconstruction, rather than query alone. A bounding-box tree or mesh can be reused; terrain edits invalidate its preparation. Existing triangulations are supplied rather than regenerated in these timings. A triangulated irregular network, bilinear elevation grid and voxel model encode different surfaces, and no solver recovers details absent from its input. Terrain-only height fields cannot represent overhangs or façades.</p>
<h3>Read measurements as measurements</h3><p>Benchmarks uses paired valid observer positions across the three supplied plan geometries and three supplied terrain meshes. Each geometry has equal requested observer coverage. Per-position medians of timed fresh-solve batches, normalized per solve, are aggregated into medians and spatial interquartile ranges. More positions improve coverage of these scenes; neither uniform sampling nor a narrow interval proves a ranking in other environments.</p><p>Resample successful observer cases with replacement inside each geometry, preserving the available geometry strata and their case counts. Recompute the median for each of 1,000 resamples; the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles define the displayed 95% bootstrap interval. This estimates sampling uncertainty in the median conditional on these fixed geometries and the measured runtime. It is distinct from the interquartile range of case values. Completed or cancelled rows with at least two successful cases can report this interval. Cancellation or failed cases can leave unbalanced geometry coverage; a partial interval describes only those available cases, with counts and failures displayed. Small samples, dependent timing noise or unrepresentative geometries limit its interpretation.</p><p>Saved results retain measurement source, runtime and date. Live worker results stream completed tests and can be stopped, leaving explicit partial coverage. Partial rows are provisional, and saved/live runtimes are not pooled. Preparation and physical-footprint recovery remain identified separately. Drawing, mismatch checks and instrumentation are excluded from timed calls. A signed test, ray intersection, raster depth evaluation and occupancy-cell visit have different costs: counts explain workload; elapsed time assesses the recorded implementation/runtime.</p>`;
const DOCS={
shadows:{title:'Construct shadow boundaries or subtract them from receivers',method:`<div class="cols"><div><h3>Construct the boundary</h3><p>For each closed box, compare its face normals with the direction toward the source. An edge joining a source-facing face to a source-away face is a <b>silhouette edge</b>. Extending its endpoints away from the source makes a shadow-volume side. Caps are needed to close that volume.</p><p>This mode explains the boundary, then paints the resulting shadows on the ground, roofs and walls. The purple source can be dragged in either view. The linked plan projects the same highlighted geometry from above; vertical faces can collapse to edges.</p></div><div><h3>Subtract shadows, facet by facet</h3><p>The second mode exposes the receiver calculation. A triangulated building facet is the current occluder. Inspect it, extend its shadow, intersect that shadow with the current candidate receiver polygons, then retain the pieces outside it.</p><p>Repeat for every building facet. Teal candidates shrink as more shadows are removed; shaded pieces have no direct connection to the source. Mutual building obstruction and self-occluded faces are included. Turn off <b>Show construction</b> for the final shaded scene.</p></div></div>`,detail:`<h3>Input, classification and extrusion</h3><p>The input consists of closed axis-aligned boxes, triangulated ground, roof and wall receivers, and movable point q. Each box face has outward unit normal n. The sign of n·(q−a), for a face vertex a, classifies it as source-facing or source-away. Edges shared by opposite classifications form the silhouette. This classification alone is not the final shadow test: another building can block a source-facing receiver.</p><p>For silhouette endpoint a, the drawn extension is a + λ(a−q). The endpoint pair and its extensions form a quadrilateral shadow side. These drawn sides are cropped to the display domain. Their finite length does not determine visibility. The silhouette mode illustrates the boundary of the closed object; receiver-subtraction modes instead show continuous source-to-facet construction rays back to q.</p><h3>Actual receiver subtraction</h3><p>Each building face is split into triangular occluders and receivers. For occluder a,b,c, three planes through q and its edges enclose an angular cone; the facet's supporting plane starts the shadow behind that facet. A plane is represented by normal n and offset d; its retained side satisfies n·p−d ≥ 0. Side normals come from (a−q)×(b−q), with signs chosen toward the cone interior. Orient the base plane so q is outside. These four half-spaces define a depth-aware convex shadow.</p><p>Start each receiver at its whole triangle. If all of its vertices lie outside one shadow half-space, it has no positive-area overlap and is retained. Otherwise clip to find the overlap. An edge crossing a plane creates p=a + f(a)/(f(a)−f(b))·(b−a), where f(p)=n·p−d. For an actual overlap, walk through the shadow planes: emit the outside polygon, carry the inside remainder to the next plane, and discard the final inside polygon. Emitted polygons retain their original receiver identifiers. Subsequent occluders process these surviving fragments.</p><p>The facet-by-facet mode records preparation, actual overlap and partition checkpoints. A facet whose shadow misses all current candidates makes no visible cut. The operation readouts count the corresponding signed tests and polygon clips across all receivers; extrusion drawing and screen projection are excluded. Repeated fragments can make the work much larger than the original face count. The original bounded receiver model also includes artificial side and ceiling boundary triangles; their subtraction work remains in the counters, although physical surface readouts include only ground, roofs and walls. This teaching implementation does not use a spatial hierarchy to remove all distant receiver–occluder pairs.</p><h3>Display, depth and precision</h3><p>Coplanar fragments are grouped by complete receiver face for display. Shared subdivision seams cancel before visible/hidden boundaries are outlined. Scalable Vector Graphics (SVG) display occlusion removes screen regions blocked by nearer opaque faces; those drawing operations never enter receiver surface area or solver counters. Orthographic depth is affine over a planar face, allowing overlap to be split where planes cross. Transparent construction layers retain centroid-depth ordering and can have approximate ordering at intersections.</p><p>Calculations use binary64 floating-point numbers. Coplanar contact with the occluder's own base plane is regularized; positive-area slivers below 10⁻⁸ are removed. Normal construction uses the whole polygon to handle collinear leading vertices. Turning construction off changes presentation, not the solved fragments. <a href="#lab-glossary">Terms and abbreviations</a>.</p>`},
expansion:{title:'Point location → shared opening → clipped triangle',method:`<h3>How the first triangle is found</h3><p>A triangle contains the observer when the observer is on its interior side for all three directed edges. These are orientation tests. The default <b>bounding-box index</b> first rejects groups of triangles whose axis-aligned bounds do not contain the point, then applies those three tests to the candidates. “Find the observer triangle” animates that search. Select a linear scan for comparison. The box index is a teaching implementation; the Computational Geometry Algorithms Library (CGAL) also offers walking and triangulation-hierarchy strategies.</p><h3>What moves during expansion?</h3><p>The observer stays fixed. Each gray mesh edge has a stored neighboring-triangle identifier. A shared free-space edge is called a <b>portal</b>, meaning an artificial opening introduced by triangulation. Following that identifier advances the calculation into the adjacent triangle.</p><p>The incoming straight-line fan clips the shared edge. Purple is the whole edge, orange is its surviving part. Extend the two orange endpoint directions <b>past the opening</b>: they bound the fan inside the next triangle. Keep the portion between them. Dark teal lines persist where a cut crosses the interior of an original triangle. A wall has no neighbor and stops that branch.</p><p>Different branches around holes may reach one triangle through disjoint openings. An unrestricted flood fill would instead reach around corners, which is not visibility.</p>`,detail:`<h3>Mesh and point-location state</h3><p>The input is a polygonal free-space domain with obstacle holes and a constrained triangulation. Every triangle is oriented counterclockwise. An edge-to-triangle map pairs matching endpoints and stores each neighbor; an unpaired edge is a domain or obstacle boundary. For directed edge a→b, the orientation value is (b−a)×(q−a). All three values at least −10⁻⁸ identify a containing triangle.</p><p>The bounding volume hierarchy (BVH) encloses triangle groups in axis-aligned boxes. It splits by the widest box axis and median primitive-center coordinate, down to at most four triangles per leaf. A point query rejects boxes not containing q, then performs triangle side tests in surviving leaves. Overlapping boxes mean several branches can survive. A linear query tests every triangle.</p><p>“Find the observer triangle” shows the recorded search only through its first successful triangle and holds there at completion. That is the displayed explanation, not an early-exit optimization of the computational core. The core gathers all incident containing triangles, needed when q lies on an artificial shared edge. Those roots seed the subsequent expansion. Obstacles are excluded by the observer-validity margin of 0.02 scene units.</p><h3>Opening propagation</h3><p>A queue item holds triangle, previous triangle, parent entry, depth, shared portal, surviving opening and inward half-planes through q. Clip the whole portal segment against the incoming half-planes. Where endpoint signed values f(a) and f(b) change sign, the intersection is a + f(a)/(f(a)−f(b))·(b−a). The two surviving endpoint directions define the next straight-line cone; clip the neighboring triangle against it.</p><p>The observer never moves with the queue. An edge behind the observer relative to the current triangle, the incoming edge, and seeded roots are not followed again. Walls end propagation. Distinct openings can enter one triangle separately, so a single global visited flag would lose visibility. Result fragments retain their parent chain and sum to visible area; cuts do not alter the input mesh. Segments shorter than 10⁻⁸ and fragments below 10⁻⁸ area are dropped, and excessive queue growth raises an error. <a href="#lab-glossary">Terms and abbreviations</a>.</p>`},
air:{title:'A 3D opening is a shared face between air cells',method:`<p>The triangulated irregular network (TIN) partitions the ground surface. To extend the free-space idea from 2D, partition the <b>air above it</b> into tetrahedra. Each triangular ground prism is divided consistently into three tetrahedra. Adjoining faces are paired during preprocessing.</p><p>Locate the observer's air cell by testing its face half-spaces. A whole shared face is purple. Intersect it with the incoming polygonal beam: the surviving opening is orange. It may be a triangle or a more complex convex polygon. The beam's side planes pass through the original observer and the opening edges.</p><p>Clip the next air cell by those planes. The resulting green polyhedron shows the reachable portion. Its new cut edges are explicitly outlined. Each outgoing shared face repeats the operation. A ground face terminates the beam and contributes a visible terrain polygon. Completed ground cuts remain visible as the process advances.</p><p>“Follow one beam” exposes one path through this branching calculation. “Accumulate all terrain beams” accumulates every branch and finishes with the full visible surface. The projected-cut mode uses this same main view and its insets to connect a selected physical terrain triangle with its surviving observer-facing footprint. Single-click a terrain triangle to select it; the selection persists in the other terrain tabs. Repeated visits to an air cell are counted separately from unique cells. The observer-detail inset centers on the current whole shared face, surviving opening or clipped air cell, depending on the stage, while always fitting the entire input face. It projects the same green filled terrain and cell edges highlighted in the main view and plan. The input face and its vertex letters are inspection overlays drawn above the terrain, so an obstruction cannot hide the construction being explained. Matching letters identify original and newly cut vertices in the main view and observer inset. Its plan inset projects the same cells and limiting rays vertically. Scroll the main view to zoom; wireframe opacity can be adjusted separately from showing the complete input mesh.</p>`,detail:`<h3>Build the air-cell graph</h3><p>The synthetic triangulated irregular network (TIN) has a single height at each horizontal position. Terrain density selects 6×4, 10×7 or 12×8 grid cells, each split into two planar terrain triangles. Each terrain triangle is extended vertically to a planar ceiling, and its prism is divided consistently into three tetrahedra. Vertex ordering gives matching subdivisions on adjacent prism faces. Matching triangular faces establish cell adjacency.</p><p>The ceiling is max(highest terrain vertex, q elevation) + max(0.25, selected clearance). A straight segment between q and any terrain point cannot rise above both endpoints, so this cap preserves all direct terrain sightlines. Clearance prevents degenerate tetrahedra at peaks; it is an analysis boundary, not a sky model. Moving q horizontally recomputes its interpolated ground height plus the selected eye height.</p><h3>Beam state and transmission</h3><p>Containment tests each tetrahedron's four outward half-spaces n·q−d ≤ 10⁻⁸; all incident containing cells are seeded. A queue entry stores cell, incoming face, parent, depth, aperture and beam side planes. Clip an outgoing shared face against the incoming beam. For aperture vertices aᵢ, side normals come from (aᵢ−q)×(aᵢ₊₁−q), oriented toward the aperture interior. Every plane still passes through q.</p><p>A surviving internal aperture sends its cone to the neighbor. A terrain boundary instead records a visible receiver polygon; a ceiling or side boundary stops at the finite domain. The clipped next-cell illustration also creates cap polygons where beam planes cut tetrahedron edges. These caps describe the current beam inside air, not new terrain faces.</p><h3>Observer inset and vertex correspondence</h3><p>The observer-detail camera stays at q and aims at the current face, opening or clipped-cell centroid. Its rectangular window fits that stage's geometry together with the entire input face, with extra room for labels, while keeping the stage centroid centered. If this direction puts an input corner behind the camera, use the observer-facing face normal instead. Project points by dividing their camera-right and camera-up coordinates by positive forward depth. Polygon and segment drawing clips at depth 0.01 and at the inset rectangle.</p><p>The green polygon fills and line segments come from the same current-stage scene as the external view and plan. The completed viewshed is not substituted for the current branch. Draw the complete input-face outline and surviving opening above terrain context as inspection overlays; this deliberate display order keeps the construction readable even when terrain would hide part of it. A shared list labels the original face vertices A, B and C, then assigns further letters to distinct new opening or target-cut vertices. Both views use those same physical points and letters, with a white text outline for readability. These camera and layering choices do not change the visibility calculation.</p><h3>Output and limits</h3><p>Output includes parent-linked beam entries and terminal polygons. Physical terrain area sums only terminal terrain fragments; boundary caps are excluded. Multiple entries can reach a cell, and each is kept. Tiny area and solid-angle apertures are rejected; growth beyond the 80,000-entry guard reports failure. Wireframes expose input cells, while completed cut edges expose query fragments. Reflections, diffraction and unrestricted traversal around corners are absent. A real urban model would need a volume decomposition conforming to buildings and overhangs, not just these vertical terrain prisms. <a href="#lab-glossary">Terms and abbreviations</a>.</p>`},
terrain:{title:'Subtract depth-aware terrain shadows from candidate surfaces',method:`<p>Choose an opaque terrain facet. Three planes through its edges and the observer define its angular shadow; a fourth plane starts that shadow at the facet. This depth condition preserves receiver geometry in front of the obstruction.</p><p><b>Only the highlighted face's shadow</b> clips every terrain receiver against that one shadow. After the extrusion, it follows real receiver-by-receiver classification, overlap and partition checkpoints. Its final teal remainder is a candidate surface: other possible occluders are deliberately ignored.</p><p><b>Subtract shadows, face by face</b> repeats a four-stage sequence for every terrain occluder: inspect the facet, construct its shadow, show actual receiver overlaps, then keep the remainder. Accepted teal candidates shrink as further shadows are removed.</p><p>Nearby-first and spiral change processing order. Each mode preserves depth through the facet base plane. Full accumulation produces the continuous terrain viewshed; the shaded and retained polygons cover physical sloping surfaces.</p>`,detail:`<h3>Define and subtract one shadow</h3><p>For opaque facet a,b,c and observer q, each edge defines an observer-through-edge side plane. Its normal is proportional to (a−q)×(b−q); orient it toward the facet's cone interior. Add the facet's base plane, oriented so q is outside and geometry behind the facet is inside. The shadow is the intersection of these four positive half-spaces n·p−d ≥ 0. Omitting the base plane would incorrectly remove foreground receivers whose projected directions merely overlap.</p><p>For every current receiver fragment, reject the shadow when all vertices lie outside any one half-space. Otherwise clip against the planes to find actual overlap. During subtraction, emit the outside portion of the remaining polygon at each plane and continue with its inside portion. Discard the final inside remainder. The emitted polygons are disjoint except along shared boundaries. Edge crossings create interpolated vertices, so the method can cut a triangle's interior even when its original vertices alone do not reveal the obstruction.</p><h3>Selected shadow and full accumulation</h3><p>One-face mode keeps only the selected occluder's operation against every receiver. A single click chooses the occluder, with that selection shared by the other terrain tabs. Automatic selection searches the near ridge for an illustrative blocking face. The single-shadow timeline reserves 5% for choosing the facet, 5% for preparing planes, 25% for continuously growing the extrusion, 50% for the recorded receiver checkpoints and 15% for the completed result. During extrusion growth the work count stays at its preparation checkpoint. Previous / Next step follows the exact start of each stage or receiver checkpoint. After constructing the shadow, the animation highlights the current receiver and follows its recorded classification, actual overlap, and retained partition. A receiver that misses the shadow still contributes its real rejection work. The teal remainder is not yet a solution accounting for every obstruction.</p><p>The accumulated mode initializes every receiver with its whole terrain triangle and subtracts every facet's shadow. It retains preparation, overlap and partition checkpoints for each occluder, using the same shadow-subtraction kernel as the selected-face mode. Dynamic counters report only work reached at the displayed checkpoint, including rejected fragment candidates. Polygon clips advance when actual tests and partitions occur; explanatory extrusion length does not invent fractional work.</p><p>Nearby-first sorts centroids by three-dimensional distance. Spiral sorts horizontal distance into outward bands and then azimuth. Neither is a general depth order. Both are valid subtraction orders because every shadow includes its base plane and every occluder is processed. In real arithmetic they remove the same union of shadows; floating-point ordering can leave small differences.</p><h3>Relation to the observer viewport</h3><p>This tab is <b>occluder-first</b>: apply one facet's shadow to all current receivers, then move to the next occluder. Terrain in the view is <b>receiver-first</b>: select a receiver clipped to the viewport, apply all occluders to it, then accept its surviving pieces and move to the next receiver. The physical shadow test is the same. The viewport tab restricts its output domain and exposes the work for that smaller domain; it does not infer depth from projected overlap alone.</p><p>All-pairs candidate work is proportional to receiver count times occluder count before fragment growth. Cheap half-space rejection avoids many full clips, but splitting increases subsequent work and storage. Normals use the whole polygon; source-on-plane, tangent and coplanar contacts are regularized. Fragments below 10⁻⁸ area are dropped. This is continuous floating-point geometry, not certified exact arithmetic. <a href="#lab-glossary">Terms and abbreviations</a>.</p>`},
projection:{title:'Build the viewport from depth-tested receiver fragments',method:`<p>First choose a target receiver. Clip its physical triangle to the observer's viewport and project that candidate. Process its terrain occluders through preparation, actual shadow-overlap detection and fragment partitioning, then accept the surviving green fragments. The final acceptance stage removes orange and purple construction geometry so the piece being added is unambiguous. A fully hidden target contributes nothing.</p><p><b>Explain the projected cut</b> stays with one selected receiver. <b>Accumulate the observer view</b> repeats the same calculation for every triangle intersecting the fixed forward viewport, including candidates that will later prove fully hidden. It processes nearby centroids first and accumulates accepted green surfaces.</p><p>The complete viewport looks along +X, with −Y on its right. In the selected-receiver mode, Target detail optionally aims the observer camera toward that face and fits its extent; turning it off uses the fixed forward viewport. Accumulation always uses the fixed +X viewport and never follows its current target. The framed viewport in the external view identifies the projected copy. Matching letters refer to the same physical vertices in both images. The orbiting illustration camera does not move the observer or change the visibility result.</p><p>This is receiver-first shadow subtraction, using the same depth-aware half-space test as Terrain: shadow cuts. The difference is traversal and output domain. Physical area is measured on surviving sloping terrain in m², not as screen occupation or air volume.</p>`,detail:`<h3>Prepare viewport candidates</h3><p>Let observer q have forward unit vector f, right r and up u. The accumulated view uses f=(1,0,0), r=(0,−1,0), u=(0,0,1). For point p, depth z=(p−q)·f and perspective coordinates are x=(p−q)·r/z, y=(p−q)·u/z. Clip before dividing by z. A near half-space enforces z ≥ 0.01; four side planes enforce xmin·z ≤ (p−q)·r ≤ xmax·z and ymin·z ≤ (p−q)·u ≤ ymax·z. Each boundary is a linear plane through q, except the offset near plane.</p><p>Clip every input receiver triangle against these five planes. Keep positive-area intersections as viewport candidates and sort their centroids by distance to q. This preparation includes triangles later blocked completely: the visibility result is not used to decide whether a candidate enters the traversal. Centroid distance is an explanatory order, not a proof that whole triangles have been depth-sorted. Occluders outside the viewport remain available: their shadow can still reach a candidate inside it.</p><h3>Process one receiver continuously</h3><p>Start from the viewport-clipped polygon on the physical terrain plane. For every terrain occluder, build three angular side planes through q and the occluder's edges, plus the occluder base plane starting its shadow behind that face. A projected overlap alone is insufficient: a foreground receiver sharing the same directions must remain visible.</p><p>Reject a shadow if all candidate-fragment vertices are outside any one half-space. Otherwise clip to obtain the actual overlap. Partition each affected fragment plane by plane, retaining outside pieces and discarding the final inside remainder. Candidate blockers under test are highlighted, including those in rejection batches. Construction rays continue back to q rather than beginning at an artificial near-plane point. Changed blockers have four explanatory stages: prepare the shadow, extend it, detect the actual overlap, and retain the partitioned remainder. Preparation and extension share one recorded work checkpoint because extension is display geometry. The trace records signed predicate evaluations and polygon passes at preparation, overlap and partition checkpoints. It exposes candidates, prepared blockers, actual overlapping shadows and retained fragments before accepting the completed target. Consecutive blockers that make no cut are grouped into display steps of at most ⌈N/8⌉ blockers, where N is the terrain occluder count. Every blocker still executes its actual rejection tests; the group holds the final cumulative checkpoint. Repeated receiver processing builds the accumulated viewport without sampling rays or raster cells.</p><p>After all occluders, accept only the surviving green physical fragments and their perspective footprints. Their owner identifiers link them to the original receiver. Hidden targets add zero area. Summing ½‖(pᵢ−p₀)×(pᵢ₊₁−p₀)‖ over polygon triangle fans yields physical area; off-viewport terrain is excluded. Perspective footprint area is in normalized screen units and is neither square meters nor automatically a solid angle.</p><h3>What differs from shadow cuts?</h3><p>Terrain: shadow cuts loops over <b>occluders, then all current receivers</b>. This tab loops over <b>viewport receivers, then all occluders</b>. They subtract the same depth-aware convex shadows with the same half-plane clipping arithmetic. Restrict the full shadow-cut result to the same viewport and their retained surfaces should agree within floating-point tolerance. The receiver traversal changes when results become available and can avoid processing off-viewport receivers; it does not introduce a different physical definition of visibility.</p><p>The lab rebuilds shadow planes during repeated receiver processing and lacks a production spatial/angular hierarchy. Counting signed tests makes the work inspectable but does not capture all plane construction, sorting, allocation or screen drawing costs. Compare complete viewport work with complete scene work only when that scope difference is intentional; use matched-output timing for performance claims.</p><h3>Inspection camera and correspondence</h3><p>The selected-target inspection camera aims f=normalize(c−q) at the target centroid c. If a corner would lie behind that forward plane, use the observer-facing face normal. With world up w=(0,0,1), r=normalize(f×w) and u=r×f; near-vertical gaze uses w=(0,1,0). Fit the entire target in the detail window; blockers and extrusion geometry can extend outside that window. This framing preserves left/right correspondence and does not change q. Turning Target detail off uses the fixed forward domain, which can exclude all or part of the selected face. Accumulation always uses a fixed forward viewport for its calculation and final image.</p><p>A projected point on the physical viewport at depth s is q+s/z·(p−q). Its rectangular frame surrounds the projected copy in the external illustration. Actual cut endpoints use shared letter labels. Coplanar fragment seams cancel before drawing cut edges; purple directions identify nearer blockers, and teal continuations reach their cut vertices. Acceptance removes these construction overlays and keeps only the green target contribution. The beam tab's projected-cut explanation uses its own beam result and omits this external projected copy.</p><p>All terrain backdrop triangles are neutral gray context, including surfaces not yet accepted. A completed visibility solution is not substituted for this backdrop. Near-plane and grazing geometry need tolerance handling; binary64 arithmetic is not exact, and tangential zero-area pieces are omitted. Display occlusion and inspection overlays are separate from solver visibility. <a href="#lab-glossary">Terms and abbreviations</a>.</p>`},
metrics:{title:'Compare geometric regions with ray and raster samples',method:`<p>Choose <b>Raycast</b> or <b>Raster</b> above the explanation. The main slider controls the actual calculation resolution. In 3D Raycast, <b>Draw rays</b> selects all calculated directions, terrain hits only, or none; this changes drawing without changing the calculation or its accuracy. The six-face inset always retains the complete hit/sky classification.</p><h3>2D planar area</h3><p>Teal is the continuous triangle-expansion region, with an independent corner-event boundary. Raycast uses 8 to 1,024 equally spaced directions and joins their first-hit endpoints into the warm Raycast-colored polygon. Its shoelace area measures that displayed polygon, not a mean radial distance.</p><p>Raster uses 8 to 128 world-grid columns and about 0.65 times as many rows. It first marks building occupancy at cell centers, then traverses the grid to classify visibility from the observer. The warm sampled region is the union of the visible query cells. This is a grid-quantized obstacle and output model, not a vector wall intersection repeated at every cell center. Thin walls or gaps can disappear or expand under that quantization.</p><p>Compare both total area and mismatched area. Missing and extra regions can cancel in the total; mismatch measures their physical symmetric difference. A raster cell and an angular interval represent different spatial resolutions even when their sample counts happen to match.</p><p>For Raycast, <b>Walls versus open boundary</b> instead compares first-hit building-wall lengths, analysis-border lengths and escaped directions. This option applies to angular rays; Raster compares cell area. The border is an artificial planar limit rather than physical sky.</p><h3>3D physical surface area</h3><p>Raster constructs a terrain visibility texture with 8 to 256 world-grid columns across the 100 m terrain and round(0.7N) rows across its 70 m width. Elevation nodes sample the original triangles and form a bilinear height surface. Each texel center receives a visibility query through that surface. Its result colors every original-triangle piece in the texel. Visible and hidden texture pieces partition the terrain; summing their sloping physical areas compares the warm Raster texture with the continuous Terrain: shadow cuts reference.</p><p>Raycast samples six observer-camera faces. N is the number of centers along one face's side, from 2 to 256. The total ray count is <b>R=6N²</b>, up to <b>393,216 rays</b>. Each first hit seeds the connected, observer-facing terrain sheet within that angular cell. Recovery crosses genuine shared triangle edges, rather than stopping at the first triangle's boundary. It stops at back-facing silhouette folds, misses and disconnected depth layers. This repairs source-triangle gaps while preserving finite angular boundary error.</p><p>The inset states R and the grid-side N explicitly. Warm cells hit terrain; pale cells see sky. Its row runs represent every center, with grid lines at readable low resolutions. Ray visibility controls the main illustration only. At very high counts, drawing all rays or all sample-cell edges can still cost more than the query itself.</p><p>Use overlay or side-by-side to compare the same scene and observer. Hidden-surface mode displays physical complements. Optional envelopes and texture masks contribute no area or volume. Raster's height grid cannot represent overhangs; Raycast still intersects the original triangles. Physical mismatch measures extra and missing surface area, rather than judging the number of rays, screen pixels or apparent coverage alone.</p>`,detail:`<h3>Planar ray reconstruction</h3><p>Let G be the continuous visible region and S the displayed sampled region. Triangle expansion gives G; an independent corner-event reference supplies its boundary. Uniform 2D directions use the centers of equal angular intervals. Intersect each with the nearest boundary edge and connect consecutive endpoints. S has shoelace area ½|Σ(xᵢyᵢ₊₁−yᵢxᵢ₊₁)|. Hits are geometric intersections, but the connections between samples introduce error.</p><p>Clip the sampled observer-centered triangle fans against continuous fragments to find |G∩S|. Missed area is |G|−|G∩S|; extra area is |S|−|G∩S|; mismatch is their sum, |G|+|S|−2|G∩S|. Equal areas can conceal a large mismatch. The enclosing analysis border is a planar limit, not physical sky.</p><p>Raycast wall coverage assigns each angular sample interval to its first-hit edge, clips that edge to the interval and sums the resulting physical wall and border lengths. Escaped-direction percentage counts sample directions reaching the enclosing border. These angular assignments remain approximate between directions and are not the area of a raster mask.</p><h3>Planar occupancy raster</h3><p>For N columns, use round(0.65N) rows over the bounded plan. Classify each grid-cell center as inside a building or free. Only free cells receive visibility queries. Starting at the cell containing the actual observer position, advance through crossed grid boundaries toward each free query-cell center; an occupied or out-of-domain cell blocks the query. When the segment passes exactly through a grid corner, test both side-adjacent cells before moving diagonally, so touching an occupied corner blocks transmission. If quantization marks the observer's own cell occupied, all of these queries are blocked at their start. The obstacles used by this method are these classified cells, rather than the original wall segments. Display every query cell with its visibility classification.</p><p>Sum the physical area of visible cells for S, and intersect their footprints with continuous fragments to measure mismatch. Building-center quantization can miss subcell obstacles, thicken others, alter narrow passages and shift the observer's effective relation to a wall. Increasing N changes both obstacle and output discretization. This independent grid traversal is not the terrain-elevation sweep described by Geographic Resources Analysis Support System (GRASS), and it is not a certified conservative obstacle mask.</p><p>Report occupancy-cell visits separately. They are discrete grid operations, not signed geometric half-plane tests or ray–wall intersection attempts. Grid preparation, query traversal and continuous-reference clipping have distinct roles; avoid treating their raw counts as equally expensive instructions.</p><h3>3D world-grid visibility texture</h3><p>For N columns across the terrain bounds, use round(0.7N) rows. Prepare each grid node by barycentric interpolation in the containing terrain triangle. Clip original triangles against each intersected XY texel rectangle to retain its physical output pieces. Cache this mesh-to-texture mapping, each piece's physical area, and each texel's center height; preparation is reusable for a fixed geometry and resolution.</p><p>In one bilinear cell, write h(u,v)=a+bu+cv+duv. Cache a,b,c,d. Substituting a straight XY sightline makes h(t) minus the sightline elevation quadratic, A t²+B t+C, within each crossed interval. A digital differential analyzer (DDA) visits every crossed X/Y cell. Evaluate the interval endpoints and, if A&lt;0, its interior maximum at −B/(2A) when that point lies inside the interval. A positive excess above 10⁻⁷ scene units blocks the target. The scalar implementation avoids repeated coordinate conversions and temporary vectors, while retaining the interior saddle test that prevents tunneling through a ridge.</p><p>Each whole texel inherits its center's result, including every mapped original-triangle fragment. Summing cached physical fragment areas gives the visible area; hidden pieces complete the terrain partition, apart from tiny discarded clipping slivers. A skyline inside a texel remains unresolved, and bilinear grid relief differs from the original piecewise-planar terrain. Resolution changes both query relief and classification footprint. Continuous shadow cuts expose both sources of mismatch.</p><p>The method counts actual crossed cells and height comparisons. These are different from ray–triangle intersections. Preparation, query and output recovery are timed separately. This is a per-target central-processor terrain-grid algorithm; it does not implement the neighboring-query reuse of the Geographic Resources Analysis Support System (GRASS) sweep or graphics-hardware rasterization.</p><h3>Angular rays and connected terrain reconstruction</h3><p>Tile six 90° cube-camera faces with N×N centers. For a face's forward f, right r and up u, a center's direction is f+αr+βu, with α,β in [−1,1]. This vector is not normalized; its ray parameter uses that same scale. A linear scan tests every triangle. A bounding volume hierarchy (BVH) first rejects bounding boxes and searches only candidate triangles, retaining the nearest hit. Both searches use the same directions and produce the same sampled hits. The query adapter flattens the reusable hierarchy, caches triangle coefficients and uses scalar intersections with a reused traversal stack. Equivalence checks preserve the original nearest-hit identifiers, depths, traversal order and operation totals.</p><p>Prepare a triangle adjacency list from actual shared mesh edges and upward-oriented triangle planes. For each cell with a hit, begin at that hit's triangle. Four planes through the observer bound the angular cell. Clip the triangle to these planes, then cross a shared edge only if a positive-length interval of that edge lies inside the cell and the neighboring triangle faces the observer. A per-cell visit stamp avoids visiting a triangle twice. Back-facing folds mark silhouettes; disconnected components and sky cells cannot seed recovery. Count face-side tests, edge/segment clipping, signed half-plane evaluations and polygon clipping separately.</p><p>The retained polygons lie on the physical input terrain, so their areas and symmetric differences can be compared with continuous visibility. They can cover several original triangles per cell. The construction does not consult the continuous answer. Coarse cells can still miss a narrow exposed island or extend around an unresolved occlusion boundary. Increasing R reduces sampling scale, but neither the sampling design nor floating-point clipping proves exact visibility.</p><h3>Can the hit points instead form a new mesh?</h3><p>Yes: organized ray-hit grids can be triangulated. The <a href="https://pointclouds.org/documentation/classpcl_1_1_organized_fast_mesh.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Point Cloud Library (PCL) organized-mesh implementation</a> includes occluded-edge, distance and maximum-edge-length safeguards. Unconditional connections can bridge a foreground object to a distant surface, connect across a silhouette, or fill sky. Even guarded hit triangles can interpolate above or below the physical terrain. Their own area would measure that reconstructed mesh, rather than visibility on the original surface. The lab therefore recovers connected original-surface pieces for its physical-area comparison. This is a teaching adaptation, not a claim to implement PCL's reconstruction algorithm.</p><h3>Drawing and numerical work</h3><p>Numerical fragments remain authoritative. For display, cancel internal sample edges within each original face and trace closed loops. Verify their even-odd filled area against the raw fragments; fall back to raw polygons if the loops disagree. One compound SVG path per face is clipped against the illustration's opaque terrain. Holes, depth occlusion and area stay intact while the number of drawing elements falls. The hidden view uses the original face plus visible rings with an even-odd fill. Neither those display masks nor ray-line toggles change measured solver output. Exact row runs in the inset also reduce drawing elements without dropping samples.</p><p>Raster and Raycast are different approximations: one queries bilinear grid relief and classifies world texels; the other queries exact input triangles and reconstructs finite angular cells. Their resolution labels and operation types are not equal budgets. Compare matching physical mismatch and query plus recovery time in <a href="#cost" data-scene="cost">Benchmarks</a>. Reusable setup is reported separately. <a href="#lab-glossary">Terms and acronyms</a>.</p>`},
cost:{title:'Benchmark tradeoffs and observer-sampling uncertainty',method:`<p>Saved statistics makes the offline graphs available immediately. It samples 50 valid observer positions per geometry across the three supplied plans and three terrain meshes. Source fingerprint, runtime and measurement date remain visible: loading these results does not measure the current browser. The build rejects saved data from mismatched computational modules.</p><p>Run in this browser creates Live results using the selected number of positions per geometry, from 1 to 1,000; Quick starts at three positions on one geometry group and Standard at 25. The seed control reproduces observer proposals. Seeded uniform proposals generate valid positions; every method in a domain sees the same cases and each geometry contributes equally. The source observer and geometry controls in the other tabs do not select this workload.</p><p>Live tests run in a background worker. Each completed test updates coverage and graphs; Stop retains a clearly labeled partial result. Missing cases are not estimated. Saved and live measurements keep their own provenance and do not mix runtimes. Partial medians can change as the run progresses.</p><p>Time vs mismatch is the default plot for comparing speed at a similar spatial error; Overview uses separate labeled method/resolution rows. Timing and mismatch axes each offer Logarithmic or Linear scales. Observer spread (quartiles) shows case variation; Median uncertainty (95%) shows the bootstrap median interval. Export results retains the selected source. Live exports include raw case and batch timings; saved exports contain compact statistics and protocol/provenance, without offline raw cases. Values at or below 10⁻⁸% are shown in an explicit zero/numerical-tolerance band on the logarithmic mismatch axis. Measurements are retained unchanged; the band is a plotting convention, not an artificial error assigned to continuous geometry. Continuous methods have named markers rather than an arbitrary sample count. Terrain detail is a separate workload and asset: its time–mismatch curves compare every enabled method across mesh levels, and its fixed-grid growth graph compares query plus recovery time against triangle count. The vertical method list controls curve visibility in both views; method colors and symbols stay consistent. Optional filled ribbons show the selected interval, and faint individual points show actual observer measurements. Plot controls above the charts select the displayed suite; the lower New browser run panel independently selects methods, workloads, resolutions, timing calibration and repetitions, bootstrap resamples and cooperative time/test budgets; saved/live and main/detail statistics remain independent. Click the legend to hide or show a method; hover over points or lines for immediate inline labels. Click to pin all labels of one curve and emphasize its legend name; click empty plot space or press Esc to clear. Selected-point details link to the corresponding demonstration.</p>`,detail:`<h3>Balanced paired spatial sampling</h3><p>The workload holds its geometry set fixed. Seeded uniform coordinate proposals are accepted only at valid observer positions. Plan proposals exclude opaque footprint interiors and invalid boundary locations. Terrain observers stand above the interpolated surface at the specified eye height. Each of the three geometries in a domain receives the same requested position count. Corresponding methods and resolutions use the same cases, permitting paired comparisons. Building receivers and restricted viewport receivers are separately named output scopes.</p><p>More observers reveal variation from openings, ridges, occlusion and changing fragment counts. They do not add new geometry families or make this teaching implementation representative of every scene. A reproducible seed fixes proposals, not processor scheduling or garbage collection. A live run can stop before all methods or geometries finish; incomplete coverage must remain visible and must not be mistaken for a balanced final comparison.</p><h3>Spatial variation versus median uncertainty</h3><p>The interquartile range (IQR) spans the 25th to 75th percentiles of per-observer case values. It describes how differently observers perform. The 95% confidence interval (CI) instead describes uncertainty in an estimated median: resample positions with replacement within each geometry, preserve the original number of cases in each stratum, recompute the combined median for each of 1,000 resamples, and take the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles of those bootstrap medians.</p><p>Keeping strata fixed makes this interval conditional on the supplied geometry set and measured runtime. It does not resample new landscapes or hardware. A one-observer geometry contains no observed spatial variation to resample; small suites can give unstable or degenerate intervals. A narrow interval is not a guarantee of universal speed or statistical significance between methods. Completed and cancelled runs can report a row interval after at least two successful cases. A cancelled or failed subset may no longer have balanced geometry counts, and absent geometries cannot contribute to its bootstrap. Partial intervals remain explicitly labeled; row case and failure counts show the available coverage. Interpret those intervals conditionally on that limited subset, rather than as the completed planned suite. The bootstrap follows the general resampling principle introduced by <a href="https://doi.org/10.1214/aos/1176344552" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Efron, Bootstrap Methods: Another Look at the Jackknife (1979)</a>; this lab's fixed-geometry stratification is its own workload design.</p><h3>Paired terrain-detail measurements</h3><p>The saved Terrain detail suite uses 25 identical physical XY observers on each mesh of 48, 192, 432 and 768 terrain triangles. All levels sample the same synthetic relief. At each XY, interpolate the current mesh's elevation and add 8 m eye height. Thus horizontal observers are paired while the represented ground surface changes with discretization. The time–mismatch graph distinguishes methods by color and symbol, and mesh levels by line pattern and thickness. The growth graph holds a selected grid size fixed and plots each enabled terrain method's query plus physical-footprint recovery time against input triangle count. Current saved Raycast measurements use N=4, 8, 16, 32, 64 and 128 per camera-face side; terrain-surface Raster uses N=8, 16, 32, 64, 128 and 256 world-grid columns. The interactive Raycast range also reaches N=256, which is outside the stored timing budget. A method without measurements at the selected size is omitted, rather than extrapolated. Angular N is a per-face side length, giving R=6N² rays; terrain-surface N is the number of columns, with round(0.7N) rows. Equal N therefore means different sample domains and counts. Read the actual counts and compare methods at similar physical mismatch, rather than equating their resolution labels.</p><p>Each mesh/method/resolution group has its own spatial quartiles and 95% bootstrap interval for its median. Do not pool levels to imply a scaling law: more triangles can change fragment growth, visible structure and indexing quality. These local teaching measurements exclude setup outside the query/recovery timers, including scene indexing, per-observer air-cell construction and precomputed shadow occluder ordering. They are not total first-query wall-clock costs, the complexity bound or measured implementation of a published algorithm. The compact extension asset retains its base computational fingerprint and separate detail-helper fingerprint; main and detail runtimes are not combined.</p><p>The beam solver has a guard at 80,000 traversal entries. Exceeding it produces an explicit failed test, with no invented zero duration or area. This protects the teaching implementation from excessive traversal; it is not a physical constraint or a published universal beam bound. Retain failure counts and diagnostics. Medians and intervals calculated from successful cases are conditional on those successes, and can understate the difficulty of a level with many failures. The denser 1,200- and 2,048-triangle pilots remain scratch diagnostics and are not part of the displayed extension.</p><h3>Preserved references and revised samplers</h3><p>The original saved main suite has 50 observers per geometry; the original Terrain detail suite has 25. Their statistics assets and hashes remain preserved. The sampler used for those measurements is frozen under its original logical source-fingerprint name. Revised 3D Raycast and terrain Raster use separate selective replacement assets, 25 paired observer-prefix positions per geometry, and their own source fingerprints, dates and runtimes. Current plots show those revised sampler rows. Unchanged geometry and 2D methods retain their original measurements. Archived results remain exportable and are never pooled with revised rows of the same name.</p><p>Angular N is the grid side on each of six faces, so the actual count is R=6N² rays. Surface N is the number of world-grid columns, with round(0.7N) rows. Index/adjacency, height-grid and texture-map preparation are measured separately and reusable for a fixed geometry/resolution; query plus recovery excludes those costs. High-resolution pilots determine which new points fit the stated illustration time budget. That budget is not an algorithmic complexity bound.</p><p>Cloud points are actual individual observer measurements. No random scatter implies measurements. Every row retains its own provenance. Saved exports contain compact summaries and individual points; live exports additionally retain raw case timings and batch metadata. Golden linear rays, orange indexed rays and raspberry Raster have distinct warm hues; geometry retains cool hues. Hover a marker for its method, resolution, mesh detail and exact measurements.</p><h3>Timing and source provenance</h3><p>Warm each query once, then calibrate bounded batches of fresh calls to approximately 5 milliseconds. Discard calibration. Take three timed batches, normalize elapsed time by solve count and retain the per-case median. Slower methods may use one solve per batch. Raw batch durations and sizes remain in JSON exports. Calls bypass illustration caches and predicate-accounting wrappers. External predicate-accounting wrappers, drawing, mismatch validation and chart layout are untimed. Samplers retain their intrinsic traversal and test counters inside the measured implementation. Setup/preparation is identified separately; supplied plan triangulations are not regenerated. A bounding volume hierarchy can be reused for a fixed geometry. Air cells and their adjacency are constructed and measured per observer because the analysis ceiling may depend on the observer elevation. The full-terrain shadow-cut query receives an observer-distance occluder order computed before timing; this ordering cost is excluded. Query + recovery axes therefore measure the named solve and physical reconstruction, rather than total first-query wall-clock time including all scene and observer setup.</p><p>The saved suite records its source/runtime/date; a live browser worker creates a new run with its own configuration and completion counts. A stopped run retains only completed test records and is labeled partial. Switching sources does not combine their samples. Browser warmup, timer quantization, allocations, worker/runtime conditions and scheduling still affect elapsed time. The interval mainly concerns sampled observer variation, not every source of timer or hardware uncertainty.</p><h3>Methods and physical output</h3><p>Measured labels match the examples: Subtract wall shadows; Expanding triangles; Terrain: beams · Accumulate all terrain beams; Terrain: shadow cuts · Subtract shadows, face by face; Terrain in the view · Build the observer view, triangle by triangle; Shadow construction · Subtract shadows, face by face; Raycast · bounding-volume hierarchy; Raycast · linear scan; Raster · occupancy grid; and Raster · terrain surface grid. The two Raycast searches share sample directions and geometric results. In three dimensions, Raycast uses six-face angular centers, then recovers connected physical terrain sheets across shared edges within each sampled cell. Terrain Raster instead queries world-texel centers on a bilinear height grid and classifies full texel pieces mapped onto the original terrain; it has a different sample domain and reconstruction. Sample counts themselves are not square meters. A viewport with zero visible reference area has undefined relative mismatch; exclude that value from error summaries and show the excluded count, while retaining its valid timings. Planar occupancy rasterization quantizes both obstacles and output.</p><p>Mismatch is physical symmetric-difference area divided by matching continuous visible-reference area. Beams are cross-checked spatially against terrain shadow cuts. Building, viewport, full-terrain and planar outputs have different receiver domains and remain separate. Floating-point regularization and tolerances remain; zero mismatch on sampled cases is not a certified proof. Graphics-hardware rasterization, optimized elevation-grid sweeps and external visibility libraries are not timed. <a href="#lab-glossary">Terms and abbreviations</a>.</p>`},
events:{title:'Candidate corners and visible boundaries',method:`<p>A <b>candidate event</b> is an input corner direction. It changes sweep bookkeeping, even when that corner lies behind another wall. A <b>visible-boundary event</b> is a candidate at which the nearest blocking wall changes.</p><p>Dashed continuations connect the nearer wall to the hidden corner that created an otherwise puzzling subdivision. The simpler display groups sectors belonging to the highlighted nearest wall; the calculation retains the candidate intervals. This distinguishes geometric events from uniform directional sampling.</p>`,detail:`<h3>Candidate directions and interval state</h3><p>For every input corner a, compute atan2(aᵧ−qᵧ,aₓ−qₓ), wrap into [0,2π), add both interval endpoints, sort and remove near-duplicates within 10⁻¹¹ radians. In a valid domain with noncrossing boundary edges, the nearest intersected edge cannot change inside an interval that contains no corner direction. A midpoint direction therefore identifies the active blocking edge for that whole interval.</p><p>Intersect the two limiting directions with that same edge to obtain its continuous visible segment. Concatenating the segments gives the polygon boundary. A hidden corner can split an interval without changing its nearest edge: that is a bookkeeping event with no new visible-wall boundary. Candidate-detail mode shows the occluded continuation to that corner; the simpler display groups sectors hitting the highlighted edge. The solver still retains its candidate intervals.</p><h3>Intersection and complexity</h3><p>A ray is q+t d and an edge is a+u(b−a). Solving their 2D cross-product equations gives t and u; retain t ≥ 0 and 0 ≤ u ≤ 1 within tolerance, then choose the smallest t. Nearly parallel pairs are rejected. All n edges are scanned in each of O(n) candidate intervals, so this reference uses O(n²) intersection work plus angle sorting. It does not maintain a balanced active-edge structure like a published O(n log n) rotational sweep.</p><p>This construction supplies an independent boundary/area check for triangle expansion. Zero-width tangential features are regularized, and repeated/collinear directions require tolerances. Unlike uniform ray sampling, representative interval rays derive from geometric corner events; increasing an arbitrary ray count is not how its boundary is refined. The illustration's rotating direction is an explanation cursor, not the observer or an extra physical light source. <a href="#lab-glossary">Terms and abbreviations</a>.</p>`}
};


'use strict';

const WORK_DETAIL_HTML=`<h3>What the operation readouts count</h3><p>“Geometric tests” is a defined workload count, not a count of processor instructions. One test means one signed side/half-plane evaluation at a point, one point–box or ray–box test, one ray–segment/triangle intersection attempt, one raster coverage-coordinate evaluation, one terrain-height comparison, or one covered-fragment depth test. Rejected candidates and repeated evaluations count. Polygon clipping tests the closing vertex again, so a pass over m vertices normally evaluates m+1 points.</p><p>“Clip passes” counts calls that clip a polygon or a segment by a half-plane set. Polygon and segment passes are reported separately: a segment is not as expensive as a many-vertex polygon. Polygon passes are individual half-plane passes; a segment pass can test multiple planes. Their signed evaluations already contribute to “Geometric tests”, so adding tests and passes would count work twice. Hover over a test total for its operation-type breakdown.</p><h3>Compare matching scopes</h3><p>Construction animations report the recorded solver operations reached at the current slider stage. Rewinding restores that earlier count; replaying or redrawing does not charge another solve. Accumulated building and terrain shadow modes record preparation, actual overlap and partition tests for each occluder. Receiver-focused viewport traces use those same checkpoint types and can group consecutive blockers making no cut. The selected terrain-shadow mode also progresses receiver by receiver through actual classification, overlap and partition work. A missed shadow performs rejection work but makes no cut. Plane/extrusion drawing is excluded, and counters never interpolate imagined fractional operations.</p><p>Building shadow subtraction processes one building facet against the original bounded scene receivers: ground, roof and wall triangles plus artificial side and ceiling boundaries. Counters include those boundaries; physical area readouts exclude them. Terrain shadow subtraction processes one terrain occluder against all current terrain receivers. A selected projected-cut explanation counts physical frustum clipping for its receiver and every occluder test against the resulting fragments. Its accumulated mode counts physical frustum clipping/filtering for all terrain input triangles plus every processed candidate receiver. The analytical frustum consists of the near plane at 0.01 forward depth and four angular side planes. Display projection and drawing are excluded. The latter's final physical output excludes off-viewport terrain. These scopes have comparable predicate types but different workloads: a physical viewport subset, the full terrain viewshed in all horizontal directions, or a bounded building receiver model. A smaller viewport total alone does not establish a faster full-scene algorithm.</p><p>The point-location demonstration counts the displayed search prefix. The independent corner-event calculation remains a reference check rather than an exposed demonstration. A beam path counts processed entries on that path, while accumulation counts every reached branch. The beam target mode counts paths reaching its receiver. Beam and triangle expansion include root containment work; explanatory substeps within one recorded entry can retain the same count. Initial target selection can report zero before receiver clipping begins.</p><p>Geometry/sample pairs count each solver separately. Angular samples include shared-edge traversal and polygon clipping needed to recover connected physical receiver sheets from sampled camera cells. The planar occupancy raster reports visited grid cells as discrete traversal work; those visits are not renamed geometric tests or ray–wall intersections. Reference hidden surfaces include geometric complement construction; the sampled comparison complement can be drawn with an even-odd texture mask without another numerical query. Error checks, auxiliary validation, illustration projection, silhouettes, outlines and optional extrusions are excluded. Surface-patch subdivision, model/mesh/adjacency and bounding-box-tree preparation are also excluded from query counts. Normal construction, area sums, allocations, sorting and other arithmetic are not fully represented by predicate totals.</p><h3>Reuse and timing</h3><p>Cached traces preserve their work checkpoints, so orbiting or zooming the illustration changes no solver count. Changing an observer, geometry or analytical viewport invalidates the corresponding trace. Wall subtraction begins with footprint removal, then adds completed shadows; its preparation readout is a subset of that total. This implementation repeats that preparation on fresh calls, although a production solver could reuse it for a fixed map. Triangle expansion starts with the embedded free-space mesh and index; their creation is not charged to each query. Receiver-first shadow traversal currently repeats shadow-plane construction and is not a fully optimized production renderer.</p><p>The measured comparison times ordinary functions after warmup. External predicate-accounting wrappers are collected separately, so their overhead is excluded from those timings. Samplers retain their intrinsic query, traversal and typed test counters inside the measured implementation. Operation types have different costs, and fragment counts alter allocations and clipping effort. Use typed counts to explain workload and matched-output elapsed time to assess speed. <a href="#lab-glossary">Terms and acronyms</a>.</p>`;
DOCS.shadows.method+=`<h3>Relation to planar wall shadows</h3><p>This tab’s receiver calculation uses geometric shadow subtraction in three dimensions. Each triangular building face defines three angular side planes and a supporting base plane behind that face. Subtract these regions from each ground, roof and wall receiver polygon. The planar wall method uses the same idea with two side lines and one base line, applied to a free-space polygon. The two tabs use different scene models and dimensions; they do not run an identical solver. The silhouette extrusion shown here explains the boundary, while the result is calculated by receiver clipping rather than stencil rendering.</p>`;
DOCS.expansion.method+=`<h3>Relation to subtracting wall shadows</h3><p>For the same planar map and observer, both methods return the same continuous visible region. Wall subtraction repeatedly cuts a candidate region by every wall shadow. Triangle expansion follows mesh adjacency only where a surviving opening remains. A wall stops a branch, and hidden regions need not be visited. Compare their current operation totals below; the useful balance depends on obstacle layout and on whether a triangulation is already available.</p>`;
